  ],
  "applies": true,
  "fixmes": [],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`src/evidence.rs#fn[validate_input]` takes `errors: &mut Vec<String>` and collects validation errors, so it is a site of `requirements.ci.errors`. The site must still run every later check that doesn't depend on a failed check's result, and it must not report one failure twice. It meets both.\n\nViolation matches:\n- **`return None` on a non-object input:** this is harmless. With no JSON object, nothing else can be checked. The function pushes an error before returning.\n- **`definition?` and `requirements?`:** both come after the independent checks have run.\n  - In the `definition` branch, `check_keys`, `string_field` and `text_field` all run before `definition?` exits.\n  - In the `requirements` branch, `check_keys`, `string_field`, the `matches` array and string-entry checks, the duplicate check and `text_field` all run before `requirements?` exits.\n  - The early exit only skips resolution, which needs the selector. A comment in the code says so.\n- **The two `return validate_resolved_input(...)` calls:** these are tail returns. Each passes `text` and `matches` as `Option`s, so each comparison runs when its recorded field is well formed. In `fn[validate_resolved_input]`, the `matches` comparison and the `text` comparison are independent `if let` blocks. A failed `matches` comparison does not stop the `text` comparison. The only early return there is a failed `resolve_input`, and both comparisons need that result.\n\nDelegation to `fn[validate_outline_input]`:\n- That function runs `check_keys` and then the `file`, `item` and `hash` field checks before it returns.\n- It skips resolution only when `file` or `item` is unusable, and it does not need `hash` for resolution.\n- It compares the hash only after a successful resolution.\n\nDuplicate reporting:\n- In `fn[validate_input]`, `check_keys` flags only unexpected keys, while `string_field` and `array_field` flag missing or mistyped keys, so no failure is reported twice.\n- In `fn[text_field]`, an empty array, a non-string element and a missing array are separate failures. Each element is checked.\n- The `matches` code in `fn[validate_input]` reports a non-string entry and a duplicate name as distinct failures.\n\nI found no gap.",
  "inputs": [
